.TH V32OPT 1 "September 2026" "v32opt 20260915-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v32opt \- optimize Vircon32 assembly code
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v32opt
.I input.asm
.RB [ \-o
.IR output.asm ]
.RB [ \-O
.IR 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | s ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR c | lua ]
.RB [ \-f name ]
.RB [ \-fno\- name ]
.RB [ \-\-dot
.IR cfg.dot ]
.RB [ \-\-trigger\-max= N ]
.br
.B v32opt
.B \-\-version
.br
.B v32opt
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B v32opt
is a modular, multi-pass optimizer for assembly written for the
.B Vircon32
fantasy console. It accepts assembly produced by the Vircon32 C
compiler,
.BR v32lua (1),
or your own hand-written assembly, as well as assembly recovered
from an already-packed CART with the Vircon32
.B DevTools
.IR unpack /
.I disassemble
commands, and rewrites it to be smaller and, where it can be,
cheaper to execute. It is meant to sit
.I between
a compiler and the assembler in an ordinary Vircon32 build chain:
.B v32opt
reads one
.I .asm
file and writes another; it never invokes the assembler,
.BR packrom (1),
or any other tool itself.
.PP
Optimization is organized as a fixed-point iteration of local
.I "peephole"
passes over a sliding window of instructions, followed (at
.B \-O2
and above) by whole-program control-flow-graph analysis, and (at
.B \-O3 )
by interprocedural function inlining. Passes may be enabled,
disabled, and capped individually, both for tuning how aggressive
the optimizer is and for isolating a pass that breaks a particular
program (see
.BR \-\-trigger\-max ,
below).
.PP
A note on Vircon32 architecture, and why so much of this tool aims
at code
.IR size :
on the Vircon32 CPU every instruction executes in exactly 1 cycle,
and there are no instruction-reactive CPU flags (Carry, Zero,
Overflow) \(em destructive comparisons such as
.B IEQ
/ \fBINE\fP directly deposit \(dq1\(dq or \(dq0\(dq in the
destination register, which conditional jumps
.RB ( JT / JF )
test directly. Consequently most algebraic simplifications are
cycle-neutral;
.B v32opt
applies them anyway to shrink the binary, reduce register
pressure, eliminate memory traffic, and keep the emitted assembly
idiomatic.
.PP
Unless
.B \-v
or
.B \-t
is given,
.B v32opt
produces no output at all on success. The optimized assembly is
written to
.IR output.asm ,
or, when
.B \-o
is not given, to the input's own name with its extension (if any)
replaced by
.IR Opt.asm ,
so
.I game.asm
becomes
.IR gameOpt.asm .
The input file is never modified in place.
.PP
The recommended invocation order places the input file first, as
shown above (the usage banner does the same). This always works;
some C libraries additionally accept options after the file name,
but that is not portable, so don't rely on it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output.asm"
Write the optimized assembly to
.IR output.asm
instead of the default
.IR <input>Opt.asm .
.TP
.BI \-O " level"
Select a preset optimization level:
.RS
.TP
.B \-O0
No optimization at all (the default).
.TP
.B \-O1
All thirteen local peephole passes (see
.BR "OPTIMIZATION LEVELS" ,
below).
.TP
.B \-O2
.B \-O1
plus common-subexpression elimination, dead-function elimination,
global constant propagation and folding over the control-flow
graph, and frame-pointer elimination in functions that never touch
.B BP
(directly or through
.BR [BP\[+-]N] ).
.TP
.B \-O3
.B \-O2
plus trivial leaf-function inlining. The most aggressive tier:
sweeping structural changes, with the highest chance of breaking a
program.
.TP
.B \-Os
Currently identical to
.B \-O3
(a placeholder for a dedicated size-focused tier).
.RE
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report the enabled passes up front, then per-iteration statistics
(pass name and hit count) as the fixed-point loop runs, and a
final total.
.TP
.B \-d
Mark every optimization directly in the output file: each removed
instruction is reproduced as a
.RS
.IP
.nf
; [DEBUG <pass>] <original instruction>
.fi
.RE
.IP
comment at its original position, and each rewritten instruction
is preceded by its original text. Combine with
.B \-\-trigger\-max
to identify exactly which transformation a bisection lands on.
Unrelated to compiler-style
.B \-g
debug info.
.TP
.B \-t
Testing mode: machine-readable summary of the run. Prints one
.I pass:count
line per pass that changed something, then
.IR total : N .
.TP
.BR \-L " mode" ", " \-\-langmode " mode"
Language mode, \(dqc\(dq (the default) or \(dqlua\(dq. Lua mode
makes passes that track values or eliminate \(dqduplicate\(dq
computation aware of v32lua's NaN-boxed type system, so that
BOXED_* tagging/untagging idioms are never folded, forwarded, or
CSE'd away as ordinary arithmetic (see
.BR "LUA MODE" ,
below).
.TP
.BI \-\-dot " cfg.dot"
Export the control-flow graph (basic blocks, their labels and
instructions, and fall-through / jump / branch / call edges) in
Graphviz DOT format. May be combined with any optimization level,
or used alone. The export always reflects the FINAL optimized
instruction list. Render with, e.g.,
.B dot \-Tpng cfg.dot \-o cfg.png .
.TP
.BI \-f name " / " \-fno\- name
Enable or disable a single pass by name, overriding the
.B \-O
preset. Recognized pass names:
.RS
.IP
.nf
peephole\-pairs, peephole\-algebra, peephole\-compiler\-myopia,
peephole\-forwarding, peephole\-jumps, peephole\-movs,
peephole\-immediates, peephole\-reduce, peephole\-shifts,
peephole\-dead\-stores, peephole\-loads, peephole\-immediate\-prop,
peephole\-jmp\-chain, cse, dce, constant\-folding,
omit\-frame\-pointers, inline, promote\-regs, promote\-leaf,
promote\-loops
.fi
.RE
.IP
The three promote-* passes are experimental and are not part of
any
.B \-O
level; enable them explicitly. See
.BR "OPTIMIZATION LEVELS" .
.TP
.BI \-finline\-max= N
Cap the size of a function still considered inlinable, in body
instructions. A function whose straight-line body is longer than
.I N
instructions is never inlined. The value is clamped to
0..32 (the candidate-table capacity).
.IR N ""
defaults to 8.
.TP
.BI \-finline\-call\-limit= N
Cap the total number of
.B CALL
sites inlined across the whole run to
.I N
(evaluated in file order); useful for bisecting an inlining
miscompile. Negative (the default) means no limit.
.TP
.BI \-finline\-exclude= name1,name2,...
Comma-separated list of function label names that must never be
inlined.
.TP
.BI \-fmax\-passes= N
Cap the iterative local-optimization loop at
.I N
fixed-point iterations (default 1000).
.TP
.BI \-\-trigger\-max= N
Global transformation budget, shared by every pass and every
fixed-point iteration: once a total of
.I N
transformations (deleted nodes, in-place rewrites, spliced
loads/stores) have committed, every later candidate is left
untouched, exactly as if it had never matched. Negative
.I N
(or omitting the flag) means unlimited. This is the primary tool
for bisecting a miscompile: re-run with increasing
.I N
until the output breaks; the
.IR N th
transform applied, in program order across all passes, is the one
to inspect, and
.B \-d
will name it in the output. The final
.B \-v
report states how many slots were used.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print version, author, and project URL, and exit immediately.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH OPTIMIZATION LEVELS
.SS "\-O1: local peephole passes"
Stateless, window-based passes that clean up redundant
instruction sequences without structural analysis:
.TP
.I peephole\-pairs
Removes adjacent instruction pairs that cancel (double
.BR BNOT ,
a
.B CIB
immediately following the destructive comparison that already
produced a clean 0/1, register-preserving
.BR PUSH / POP
spans with nothing protective in between, identical XOR pairs).
.TP
.I peephole\-algebra
Removes self-moves and identity arithmetic; strength-reduces
multiplication by 2 into self-addition.
.TP
.I peephole\-compiler\-myopia
Removes a reload of a value that was just stored from the same
register to the same memory location.
.TP
.I peephole\-forwarding
Replaces a load from memory with a register move when the value
was just stored there from that register (read-modify-write
memory ops, aliasing stores through other base registers, and
MOVS/SETS all interrupt the match).
.TP
.I peephole\-jumps
Removes jumps to the label that immediately follows, inverts
branch-over-jump pairs
.RB ( JF + JMP
to
.BR JT ),
and eliminates code made unreachable by an unconditional jump
(not for computed targets:
.B "JMP R0"
or
.BR "JMP 0x1000" ).
.TP
.I peephole\-movs
Removes duplicate and mirror
.B MOV
pairs (same source and destination twice, or two registers swapped
back).
.TP
.I peephole\-immediates
Combines consecutive immediate additions/subtractions on the same
register into one; removes pairs that cancel (integers only \-
floating-point addition is not associative).
.TP
.I peephole\-reduce
Strength reduction: multiplication by 0/1/2, division by 1, and
modulus by 1 for integers; the exact \(mu1.0 and \(de1.0 identities
for floats (float \(mu0.0 is deliberately left alone: Inf/NaN
times zero is NaN, not zero).
.TP
.I peephole\-shifts
Removes no-op shifts and turns left-shift by 1 into
self-addition.
.TP
.I peephole\-dead\-stores
Removes stores to registers or memory that are overwritten before
any possible read (only a pure
.B MOV
store counts as an overwriter; read-modify-write instructions
read the old value and block the match).
.TP
.I peephole\-loads
Replaces a second consecutive load from the same memory address
with a register move from the first load's destination.
.TP
.I peephole\-immediate\-prop
Folds a constant
.B MOV
into a following immediate arithmetic instruction on the same
register, and merges sequential immediate arithmetic (never
across a read of the register or a conditional branch).
.TP
.I peephole\-jmp\-chain
Short-circuits jump indirection: a jump to a label that
immediately begins another unconditional jump is retargeted to
that jump's destination; the intermediate jump is removed only
when nothing else can reach its label.
.SS "\-O2: global analysis"
.B \-O1
plus:
.TP
.I cse
Common-subexpression elimination within basic blocks: a repeated
(register, operation, operand) computation is replaced by a move
from the first result \em source operands must be unchanged
between the two occurrences.
.TP
.I dce
Dead-function elimination: reachability from the boot vector,
entry points, interrupt handlers, and
.I pointer
directives; unreached functions are swept away.
.TP
.I constant\-folding
Lattice-based constant propagation over the control-flow graph,
folding known constants across jumps and invalidating
conservatively around calls. Note this trades words for immediates
(a folded
.B "MOV Rd, const"
occupies two words where the register form took one), so it can
grow the assembled binary even as it shrinks the source \em its
payoff is enabling further immediate-based peepholes.
.TP
.I omit\-frame\-pointers
Strips the
.BR "PUSH BP" / "MOV BP, SP"
prologue and
.BR "MOV SP, BP" / "POP BP"
epilogue from functions whose bodies never reference
.B BP
at all \em directly
.I or
through
.BR [BP\[+-]N] .
Deliberately a
.B \-O2
(not
.BR \-O1 )
pass: it erases the frame chain that emulators and debuggers walk
to produce backtraces, so
.B \-O1
stays debug-safe.
.SS "\-O3: interprocedural"
.B \-O2
plus:
.TP
.I inline
Trivial leaf-function inlining: short, branch-free, call-free
function bodies are spliced directly into their call sites, with
parameter accesses rewritten from the callee's
.B [BP+N]
frame to
.B [SP+N\-2]
at the call site (a frame-layout invariant: the callee's BP always
sits two words below the caller's SP at the call). Candidate
bodies may not touch SP or BP, and the spliced body runs with SP
exactly where the
.B CALL
would have left it. Control its aggressiveness with
.BR \-finline\-max ,
.BR \-finline\-call\-limit ,
and
.BR \-finline\-exclude .
.SS "Experimental (no \-O level)"
The three memory-to-register promotion passes
.RI ( promote\-regs ,
.IR promote\-leaf ,
.IR promote\-loops )
rewrite frequently accessed
.B [BP\-N]
stack slots into unused general-purpose registers, injecting
loads and flushes at region boundaries. They are not enabled by
any preset level while undergoing testing; enable them only
explicitly, and expect to have to bisect.
.SH LUA MODE
When optimizing
.BR v32lua (1)
output, pass
.BR \-L lua .
v32lua represents Lua values as IEEE-754 NaN-boxed words whose tag
bits are manipulated with
.I BOXED_*
immediate operands (OR to box, AND to unbox, IADD to box a
boolean). In C mode such instructions look like ordinary
arithmetic on unknown constants; in Lua mode the value-tracking
and duplicate-computation passes
.RI ( cse ,
.IR constant\-folding ,
.I peephole\-algebra )
recognize and protect the boxing idioms, and the algebra pass
additionally drops the provably-dead \(dqis it Nil?\(dq half of
the compiler's fixed six-instruction truthiness test after a
boxed-boolean producer.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The input was read and the optimized assembly written
successfully.
.TP
.B 1
No input file was given; an option was unrecognized or malformed;
the input file could not be opened; or the requested optimization
level or language mode does not exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
Optimize a compiler-emitted file at the safest level, writing
.IR gameOpt.asm :
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32opt game.asm \-o gameOpt.asm \-O1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Full pipeline with per-pass statistics:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32opt game.asm \-O2 \-v
.fi
.RE
.PP
Granular control: global analysis without inlining, but with the
experimental loop promotion enabled:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32opt game.asm \-O2 \-fno\-inline \-fpromote\-loops
.fi
.RE
.PP
Optimize v32lua output with boxed-type awareness:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua \-o game.asm game.lua
v32opt game.asm \-o gameOpt.asm \-O1 \-L lua
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bisect a miscompile: find the exact Nth transformation that
breaks the program, then inspect the named culprit in the output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32opt game.asm \-o gameOpt.asm \-O3 \-\-trigger\-max=137 \-d
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export and render a control-flow graph of the optimized program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32opt game.asm \-O2 \-\-dot cfg.dot
dot \-Tpng cfg.dot \-o cfg.png
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.asm
Input: Vircon32 assembly, from a compiler, hand-written, or
disassembled from a packed CART.
.TP
.IR <input> Opt.asm
Default output: the optimized assembly, ready for the Vircon32
assembler.
.TP
.IR *.dot
Optional, with
.BR \-\-dot :
Graphviz control-flow-graph export.
.PP
Note for the CART build: your cartridge XML references the
.I .vbin
produced from the assembly, so after assembling
.I gameOpt.asm
either rename the resulting
.I .vbin
or point the XML at it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32lua (1),
the Vircon32 DevTools documentation
.RI ( compile ,
.IR assemble ,
.IR packrom ,
and the
.IR unpack / disassemble
pair for recovering assembly from packed CARTs).
.PP
Project repository:
.I https://github.com/wedge1020/v32opt
.SH BUGS
This is early, actively developed software. The window passes
model read-modify-write memory instructions, aliasing stores, and
the dynamic-memory MOVS/SETS operations, but some structural
assumptions remain: unreachable-code elimination does not reason
about computed jump targets
.RB ( "JMP R0" / "JMP 0x1000" ),
DCE's boundary heuristic can confuse a real function whose name
ends in
.BR _return ,
and the experimental promote-* passes are still undergoing
testing. Treat
.B \-O3
(and the promote-* passes) as genuinely risky, and even
.BR \-O1 / \-O2
output as something to verify by assembling, packing, and running
both the original and optimized CARTs side by side. When a program
breaks,
.B \-\-trigger\-max
plus
.B \-d
will isolate the exact offending transformation, and
.B \-fno\-<name>
will retire the offending pass.
.PP
Report issues, with the input assembly and the exact flags used,
via the project repository above.
